      "spreadId": 2,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 5,
        "runningHead": "DSC Application, Duties & Revocation (Sec 35, 37, 38, 41, 42)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 3 • Digital Signatures & Corporate Liability</span>\n      <h2>Digital Signature Certificates & Subscriber Duties</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Application for Digital Signature Certificate (Section 35)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 35 of the IT Act</strong>, any person may make an application to a licensed <strong>Certifying Authority (CA)</strong> for the grant of a Digital Signature Certificate (DSC), accompanied by the prescribed fee and a certification practice statement. The CA must verify that the applicant holds the private key corresponding to the public key listed.</p>\n\n    <h3>Duties of Subscriber Regarding Private Key (Section 42)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 42</strong>, every subscriber shall <strong>exercise reasonable care to retain control of the private key</strong> corresponding to the public key listed in his Digital Signature Certificate and take all steps to prevent its disclosure to a person not authorized to affix the digital signature of the subscriber. If the private key has been compromised, the subscriber must communicate this without delay to the CA.</p>\n\n    <h3>Revocation & Suspension of DSC (Sections 37 & 38)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 38</strong>, a Certifying Authority <strong>shall revoke</strong> a Digital Signature Certificate in mandatory circumstances, including:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Upon the death of the subscriber;</strong></li>\n      <li><strong>Upon the dissolution of the firm</strong> or winding up of the company where the subscriber is a firm or company; or</li>\n      <li><strong>Upon the insolvency</strong> of the subscriber.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 37</strong>, a CA may suspend a DSC for <strong>a period not exceeding 15 days</strong> if the subscriber requests it or if the CA is of the opinion that the certificate should be suspended in the public interest. No certificate can be suspended for longer than 15 days without giving the subscriber a reasonable opportunity of showing cause.</p>\n\n    <h3>Acceptance of DSC (Section 41)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 41</strong>, by accepting a Digital Signature Certificate, the subscriber certifies to all who reasonably rely on the information that: (a) the subscriber holds the private key corresponding to the public key listed in the certificate; and (b) all representations made by the subscriber to the Certifying Authority are true.</p>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 6,
        "runningHead": "Penalties, Overriding Effect & Companies (Sec 71, 73, 74, 81, 85)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Statutory Penalties & Corporate Liability</span>\n      <h2>Fraudulent Certificates, Overriding Effect & Section 85</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Penalties Concerning Certificates (Sections 71, 73, 74)</h3>\n    <p>The statute imposes severe criminal penalties for fraud involving certificates:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 71:</strong> Penalty for misrepresentation or suppressing any material fact from the CCA or CA for obtaining any license or DSC: <strong>imprisonment for a term which may extend to 2 years, or fine up to ₹1 Lakh, or both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 73:</strong> Penalty for publishing a Digital Signature Certificate false in certain particulars: <strong>imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to ₹1 Lakh, or both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 74:</strong> Publication for fraudulent purpose (knowingly creating, publishing or making available a DSC for fraudulent or unlawful purpose): <strong>imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to ₹1 Lakh, or both</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Overriding Effect of IT Act (Section 81)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 81</strong>, the provisions of the IT Act have <strong>overriding effect notwithstanding anything inconsistent contained in any other law</strong> for the time being in force. <em>Crucial Exception:</em> The proviso to Section 81 expressly clarifies that nothing in the IT Act shall restrict any person from exercising any right conferred under the <strong>Copyright Act, 1957 or the Patents Act, 1970</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>Offences by Companies (Section 85)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 85</strong>, where an offense under the IT Act is committed by a company: <strong>the company itself, as well as every person who at the time the contravention was committed was in charge of and responsible to the company for the conduct of its business, shall be deemed guilty</strong> of the contravention and liable to be proceeded against and punished accordingly.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"exam-trap-box\">\n      <strong>DSSSB Exam Trap:</strong> Remember the twin exceptions in Section 81! The IT Act overrides almost all other statutes, but it explicitly yields to the <strong>Copyright Act, 1957</strong> and the <strong>Patents Act, 1970</strong>. Under Section 85, a director can escape criminal liability only if they prove that the offence took place without their knowledge or that they exercised all due diligence.\n    </div>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 3</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q21–Q30) on DSC duties, penalties, Section 81 and Section 85.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(2)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q21–Q30) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
