    {
      "spreadId": 8,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 17,
        "runningHead": "Examiner of Evidence (Sec 79A), Police Powers & SSMI",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 9 • Digital Evidence & Forensic Certification</span>\n      <h2>Examiner of Evidence (Sec 79A), Police Powers & SSMI</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Notification of Examiner of Electronic Evidence (Section 79A)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 79A of the IT Act</strong>, the Central Government notifies accredited central government forensic laboratories as <strong>Examiners of Electronic Evidence</strong> to specify categories of digital evidence and provide expert opinions.</p>\n\n    <h3>Emergency Police Powers (Section 80)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 80</strong>, any police officer not below the rank of <strong>Inspector</strong> may <strong>enter any public place, search, and arrest without warrant</strong> any person reasonably suspected of having committed or being about to commit any offence under the Act.</p>\n\n    <h3>Significant Social Media Intermediaries (SSMI)</h3>\n    <p>Under the IT Intermediary Rules 2021, an intermediary is classified as a Significant Social Media Intermediary (SSMI) if it has <strong>50 Lakhs (5 million) or more registered users in India</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>BSA 2023 Section 63 & Expert Opinion Section 39</h3>\n    <p><strong>Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 (BSA)</strong> replaces Section 65B of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 for the admissibility of electronic records. Under <strong>Section 39 of the BSA 2023</strong> (replacing Section 45A IEA), the opinion of an Examiner of Electronic Evidence notified under Section 79A is an admissible expert fact.</p>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 18,
        "runningHead": "Section 63(4) BSA & The Arjun Panditrao Precedent",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Electronic Evidence Jurisprudence</span>\n      <h2>Section 63(4) BSA & The Arjun Panditrao Precedent</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Section 63(4) BSA Responsible Official Certificate</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 63(4) of the BSA 2023</strong>, an electronic record certificate must be signed by <strong>a person occupying a responsible official position in relation to the operation of the relevant device or management of the relevant activities</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao Gorantyal (2020)</h3>\n    <p>In this landmark 3-judge bench ruling ((2020) 7 SCC 1), the Supreme Court established definitive rules on electronic evidence:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Indispensable Condition Precedent:</strong> A certificate under Section 65B(4) (now Section 63(4) BSA) is an <strong>indispensable condition precedent for the admissibility of secondary electronic evidence</strong> (printouts, CDs, copies).</li>\n      <li><strong>Original Device Exception:</strong> If the original electronic device itself (e.g. suspect's actual phone, camera, or original DVR) is produced in court by the owner/custodian, <strong>no certificate under Section 65B/63 is required</strong>!</li>\n      <li><strong>Complete Code:</strong> Reaffirmed <em>Anvar P.V. v. P.K. Basheer (2014)</em> that Section 65B is a complete code overriding general evidence provisions.</li>\n      <li><strong>Overruling Shafhi Mohammad:</strong> Expressly overruled the temporary relaxed exception in <em>Shafhi Mohammad (2018)</em> that a certificate was optional where a party was not in possession of the device.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <div class=\"exam-trap-box\">\n      <strong>DSSSB Exam Trap:</strong> Note the key evidentiary holding in Arjun Panditrao Khotkar:\n      <ul>\n        <li>Secondary copies (CDs, flash drives, printouts): Certificate is <strong>mandatory</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Original physical device produced directly: Certificate is <strong>NOT required</strong>!</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 9</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q81–Q90) on Section 79A, Section 80 police powers, and Arjun Panditrao Khotkar.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(8)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q81–Q90) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 9,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 19,
        "runningHead": "Evidential Status of Expert Reports & BNS 2023 Replacements",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 10 • Evidence Status & BNS 2023 Offences</span>\n      <h2>Evidential Status of Expert Reports & BNS 2023 Replacements</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Evidential Status of Section 79A Examiner Reports</h3>\n    <p>Under Section 39 BSA / Section 45A IEA, an opinion submitted by an Examiner of Electronic Evidence notified under Section 79A is <strong>expert testimony which is admissible and carries high evidential value, but is rebuttable and not conclusive proof</strong>. The defense retains the right to cross-examine the examiner on hash validation and tool integrity.</p>\n\n    <h3>Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) 2023 Penal Mappings</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Cheating (replaces IPC 420):</strong> Codified in <strong>Section 318(4) of BNS 2023</strong> (imprisonment up to 7 years and fine).</li>\n      <li><strong>Cheating by Personation (replaces IPC 419):</strong> Codified in <strong>Section 319 of BNS 2023</strong> (imprisonment up to 5 years, or fine, or both).</li>\n      <li><strong>Forgery (replaces IPC 463, 464, 465):</strong> Codified in <strong>Sections 336, 338, and 336(3) of BNS 2023</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Organized Cyber Crime:</strong> Codified specifically under <strong>Section 111 of BNS 2023</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Insult to Modesty of Women (replaces IPC 509):</strong> Codified under <strong>Section 79 of BNS 2023</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 20,
        "runningHead": "Suhas Katti, Puttaswamy & DPDP Act 2023 Penalties",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Landmark Cases & DPDP Act 2023</span>\n      <h2>Suhas Katti, Puttaswamy & DPDP Act 2023 Penalties</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Landmark Judicial Decisions</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>State of Tamil Nadu v. Suhas Katti (2004):</strong> Historic significance: it was <strong>the first case in India resulting in a conviction under Section 67 of the IT Act</strong> (obscene message posted in Yahoo groups). Conviction achieved in 7 months.</li>\n      <li><strong>Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017):</strong> 9-judge Constitution Bench held that: <strong>the Right to Privacy is a fundamental right protected as an intrinsic part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21</strong> of the Constitution.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act)</h3>\n    <p>India's standalone comprehensive personal data protection legislation is officially titled the <strong>Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act, 2023)</strong>. Under the Schedule to the DPDP Act, the maximum financial penalty that can be imposed by the Data Protection Board for a significant personal data breach is <strong>up to ₹250 Crores</strong>.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"exam-trap-box\">\n      <strong>DSSSB Exam Trap:</strong> BNS Section numbers to memorize:\n      <ul>\n        <li>Cheating (old 420): <strong>Section 318(4) BNS</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Personation (old 419): <strong>Section 319 BNS</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Organized Cyber Crime: <strong>Section 111 BNS</strong>.</li>\n        <li>DPDP Act Maximum Penalty: <strong>₹250 Crores</strong>!</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 10</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q91–Q100) on Section 79A evidence, BNS 2023 mappings, and DPDP Act penalties.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(9)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q91–Q100) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 10,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 21,
        "runningHead": "DPDP Act Entities, RBI Circular & Helpline 1930",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 11 • Data Roles & Financial Fraud Reporting</span>\n      <h2>DPDP Act Entities, RBI Circular & Helpline 1930</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>DPDP Act 2023 Statutory Entities</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Data Principal (Q101):</strong> The individual to whom the personal data relates (or parents/guardians in the case of a child or person with disability).</li>\n      <li><strong>Data Fiduciary (Q102):</strong> Any person who alone or in conjunction with other persons determines the purpose and means of processing of personal data.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>RBI 2017 Customer Liability Circular (Q103)</h3>\n    <p>Under the RBI's 2017 Circular on Limiting Customer Liability in Unauthorized Electronic Banking Transactions: if customer notifies the bank within 3 working days of receiving communication from the bank regarding unauthorized transaction, customer has <strong>Zero Liability (the bank must credit the disputed amount to the customer's account within 10 working days)</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>National Cybercrime Helpline 1930 & Portal (Q104)</h3>\n    <p>The Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) established <strong>Helpline 1930</strong> and the portal <strong>cybercrime.gov.in (National Cybercrime Reporting Portal / CFCFRMS)</strong> for immediate real-time reporting of financial cyber fraud and freezing of stolen funds.</p>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 22,
        "runningHead": "Encryption (Sec 84A), Inchoate Offences & Confiscation (Sec 76-77)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Encryption, Inchoate Crimes & Property</span>\n      <h2>Encryption (Sec 84A), Inchoate Offences & Confiscation (Sec 76-77)</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Encryption & Inchoate Crimes (Sections 84A, 84B, 84C)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 84A:</strong> Empowers the Central Government <strong>to prescribe modes or methods of encryption for secure use of the electronic medium</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 84B (Abetment):</strong> Whoever abets any offense punishable under the Act shall <strong>be punished with the punishment provided for the offense itself</strong> if the abetted act is committed in consequence.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 84C (Attempt):</strong> Whoever attempts to commit any offense under the Act shall be punished with <strong>imprisonment for a term which may extend to one-half of the longest term</strong> provided for that offense, or fine, or both.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Confiscation & Non-Bar of Other Punishments (Sections 76 & 77)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 76 Confiscation:</strong> Any computer, computer system, floppies, compact disks, tape reels or accessories used in contravention are subject to confiscation.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 76 Proviso (Innocent Owners):</strong> Computer media belonging to an innocent person <strong>shall not be confiscated</strong> if the owner proves it was used without their knowledge or consent.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 77 Non-Bar:</strong> Penalties or confiscation under the IT Act <strong>do not bar prosecution or punishment under other laws</strong> (e.g. BNS).</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 11</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q101–Q110) on DPDP Act entities, RBI 1930, Section 84A-C, and Sections 76-77.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(10)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q101–Q110) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 11,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 23,
        "runningHead": "Compounding (Sec 77A), Public Servants & Advisory Committee",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 12 • Procedural Powers & Advisory Bodies</span>\n      <h2>Compounding (Sec 77A), Public Servants & Advisory Committee</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Compounding of Offences (Section 77A)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 77A</strong>, offences under the IT Act can be compounded by a court of competent jurisdiction provided that the offence is: <strong>any offence for which the punishment is other than imprisonment for life or imprisonment for a term exceeding 3 years</strong> (first-time offenders only; repeat offenders and cyber terrorism barred).</p>\n\n    <h3>Public Servants & Immunity (Sections 82 & 84)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 82:</strong> The Controller, Adjudicating Officers, and other officers are deemed to be <strong>Public Servants within the meaning of Section 21 IPC / Section 2(28) BNS 2023</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 84:</strong> Grants statutory immunity: <strong>no suit, prosecution or legal proceeding lies against government officials for anything done or intended to be done in good faith</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>CRAC & Regulations (Sections 88 & 89)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 88:</strong> The Central Government constitutes the <strong>Cyber Regulations Advisory Committee</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 89:</strong> The <strong>Controller of Certifying Authorities (CCA)</strong> possesses the power to make regulations consistent with the Act with previous approval of the Central Government.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 24,
        "runningHead": "Intermediary Timelines & GAC",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Intermediary Timelines & GAC</span>\n      <h2>IT Intermediary Rules 2021 Timelines, Traceability & GAC</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>IT Intermediary Rules 2021 Strict Compliance Timelines</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Rule 3(2)(b) Nudity Takedown:</strong> An intermediary must remove or disable access to non-consensual nudity, sexual acts, or morphed images <strong>within 24 hours of receipt of the complaint</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Grievance Acknowledgment:</strong> Intermediaries must acknowledge receipt of any user grievance <strong>within 24 hours</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Government / Judicial Orders:</strong> Statutory deadline for an intermediary to provide information or assistance to authorized government agencies is <strong>within 72 hours of receipt of the order</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Originator Traceability & GAC (Rules 4(2) & 3A)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Traceability (Rule 4(2)):</strong> Imposes duty on Significant Social Media Intermediaries providing messaging services to <strong>enable identification of the first originator of the information</strong> upon judicial or lawful order.</li>\n      <li><strong>Grievance Appellate Committee (GAC):</strong> An independent appellate body created under the 2022 amendments to hear user appeals against decisions of an intermediary's Grievance Officer.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 12</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q111–Q120) on Compounding Section 77A, IT Rules 2021 deadlines, and GAC.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(11)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q111–Q120) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 12,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 25,
        "runningHead": "GAC Timelines, 180-Day Retention & Electronic Records",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 13 • GAC Timelines & Log Retention Standards</span>\n      <h2>GAC Timelines, 180-Day Retention & Electronic Records</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Grievance Appellate Committee (GAC) Timelines</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Appeal Filing Window (Q121):</strong> An aggrieved user must file an appeal before the GAC <strong>within 30 calendar days</strong> from the date of receipt of communication from the Grievance Officer.</li>\n      <li><strong>Appeal Resolution Window (Q122):</strong> The GAC must endeavor to resolve the appeal <strong>within 30 calendar days</strong> from the date of receipt of the appeal.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>180-Day Log Retention Mandates (Rule 3(1)(g) & CERT-In)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Rule 3(1)(g) IT Rules 2021 (Q123):</strong> An intermediary must retain registration records and user account information <strong>for a period of 180 days following cancellation or withdrawal of registration</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>CERT-In Directions 2022 (Q124):</strong> All covered organizations must mandatorily enable and securely maintain system logs for <strong>a rolling period of 180 days</strong> within Indian jurisdiction.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 26,
        "runningHead": "Sections 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 & 14 of the IT Act, 2000",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Legal Recognition of Electronic Records</span>\n      <h2>Sections 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 & 14 of the IT Act, 2000</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Statutory Recognition of Electronic Transactions</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 4 (Legal Recognition of Electronic Records):</strong> Satisfies requirement of law that information be in writing if: <strong>the information is rendered or made available in an electronic form and accessible so as to be usable for a subsequent reference</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 5 (Legal Recognition of Electronic Signatures):</strong> Satisfies requirement that a document bear a signature if authenticated by an electronic signature.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 6 (Use in Government):</strong> Recognizes <strong>the filing of forms, applications, and creation, retention, or payment of money in government agencies electronically</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 7 (Electronic Document Retention):</strong> Retention of documents satisfied electronically if: (1) accessible for subsequent reference; (2) retains original format/layout; and (3) details identifying origin, destination, date and time of dispatch/receipt are retained.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 9 (No Right to Compel):</strong> Can a citizen compel a government department or ministry to accept electronic filings as a matter of right? <strong>No, Section 9 clarifies that Sections 6, 7 and 8 do not confer a right to compel any Ministry or Department to accept electronic records</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 14 (Secure Electronic Record):</strong> An electronic record that has been subjected to a prescribed or agreed security procedure at a specific point in time is deemed a secure electronic record.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 13</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q121–Q130) on GAC timelines, 180-day logs, and Sections 4-9, 14 legal recognition.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(12)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q121–Q130) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 13,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 27,
        "runningHead": "Secure Signatures, Security Procedures & Licensing (Sec 15, 16, 19, 21)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 14 • Security Procedures & Key Management</span>\n      <h2>Secure Signatures, Security Procedures & Licensing (Sec 15, 16, 19, 21)</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Secure Digital Signatures (Section 15)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 15 of the IT Act</strong>, an electronic signature is deemed secure if: <strong>the signature creation data was, at the time of signing, under the exclusive control of the signatory and no other person, and any alteration to the electronic signature or record made after the time of affixing is detectable</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>Security Procedures (Section 16)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 16</strong>, the <strong>Central Government</strong> is empowered to prescribe security procedures and practices having regard to commercial circumstances, nature of transactions, and state of technology.</p>\n\n    <h3>Foreign CAs & CA Licenses (Sections 19 & 21)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 19:</strong> The Controller possesses power to recognize any foreign Certifying Authority as a Certifying Authority for the purposes of the Act with prior approval of the Central Government.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 21:</strong> A license granted to a Certifying Authority is <strong>valid for the prescribed period, is non-transferable, and subject to specified terms and conditions</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 28,
        "runningHead": "Search Powers (Sec 28), Section 30 CPS, Key Entitlement & Scenarios",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Search Powers, CPS & Threat Scenarios</span>\n      <h2>Search Powers (Sec 28), Section 30 CPS, Key Entitlement & Scenarios</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Controller Investigation & Repository (Sections 28, 30, 34, 40)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 28 (Search Powers):</strong> Empowers the Controller or authorized officer to <strong>access any computer system, apparatus, data, and search any premises where computer access is suspected</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 30 (CA Duties):</strong> Every CA must <strong>adhere strictly to its published Certification Practice Statement (CPS) and use trustworthy systems</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 34 (Disclosure):</strong> Controller and CAs must maintain a repository of all issued, suspended, and revoked Digital Signature Certificates, including Certificate Revocation Lists (CRL).</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 40 (Key Entitlement):</strong> Subscriber represents to the CA and relying parties that <strong>the subscriber is entitled to use the key pair and the public key corresponds to the private key</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Forensic Cyber Threat Scenarios</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Ransomware SQL Attack (Q139):</strong> Injecting ransomware, encrypting SQL databases, and extorting bitcoins: prosecutable under <strong>Section 43(c) & 43(i) read with Section 66 (Hacking) and Section 66F (Cyber Terrorism)</strong> if impacting critical infrastructure.</li>\n      <li><strong>Phishing Banking Portal (Q140):</strong> Cloning a banking website, sending lure SMS, and harvesting OTPs: prosecutable under <strong>Sections 66C and 66D of the IT Act read with Section 319 (Cheating by personation) of BNS 2023</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 14</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q131–Q140) on Secure signatures Section 15, Section 28 search, and ransomware/phishing.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(13)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q131–Q140) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 14,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 29,
        "runningHead": "Insider Exfiltration, Protected Systems & Landmark Cases",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 15 • Insider Exfiltration & Judicial Precedents</span>\n      <h2>Insider Exfiltration, Protected Systems & Landmark Cases</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Forensic Scenarios & Protected Systems</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Git Repo Exfiltration (Q141):</strong> A disgruntled engineer exfiltrating proprietary source code to a personal USB drive violates <strong>Section 43(b) read with Section 66 of the IT Act, Section 65 (Source code), and Section 72A (Contractual breach)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Protected Systems (Section 70) (Q142):</strong> When declaring a system as Protected, the government order <strong>may authorize the specific persons who are permitted to access the protected system</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Enterprise Cloud Server Logs (Q143):</strong> Cloud server logs and database printouts require a <strong>Section 63 BSA / 65B IEA certificate because they are secondary electronic records produced by a computer system</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Landmark Judicial Precedents</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>State (NCT of Delhi) v. Navjot Sandhu (2005) (Q144):</strong> In the Parliament attack case, the Supreme Court <strong>incorrectly held that secondary electronic evidence could be proved by oral testimony under Section 63/65 without a Section 65B certificate</strong> (later formally overruled by <em>Anvar P.V.</em> in 2014 and <em>Arjun Panditrao</em> in 2020).</li>\n      <li><strong>Syed Asifuddin v. State of Andhra Pradesh (2005) (Q145):</strong> Altering the Electronic Serial Number (ESN) / IMEI of a mobile phone constitutes <strong>tampering with computer source code under Section 65 of the IT Act</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>SMC Pneumatics (India) Pvt. Ltd. v. Jogesh Kwatra (2001) (Q146):</strong> India's first case involving cyber defamation where an <strong>injunction was granted restraining an ex-employee from sending derogatory emails</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 30,
        "runningHead": "ISO/IEC 17025, Chain of Custody & BSA 2023 Sec 63(2)(a)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Laboratory Accreditation & Evidentiary Rules</span>\n      <h2>ISO/IEC 17025, Chain of Custody & BSA 2023 Sec 63(2)(a)</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>ISO/IEC 17025 Accreditation (Section 79A) (Q147)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 79A of the IT Act</strong>, a digital forensic laboratory seeking notification as an Examiner of Electronic Evidence must hold <strong>ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation (General requirements for the competence of testing and calibration laboratories)</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>Expert Cross-Examination & Chain of Custody</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Cross-Examination (Q148):</strong> The defense has full statutory right to summon and cross-examine an Examiner of Electronic Evidence under Section 79A on tool calibration, hashing baseline, and analysis.</li>\n      <li><strong>Broken Chain of Custody (Q149):</strong> Seizing mobile phones without Faraday shielding and storing without cryptographic hashes establishes <strong>a serious breach of Chain of Custody, creating reasonable doubt regarding tampering</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>BSA 2023 Section 63(2)(a) Regular Use Condition (Q150)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 63(2)(a) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023</strong>, the primary condition for electronic record admissibility is that: <strong>the computer was used regularly to store or process information for the purposes of any activities regularly carried on over that period by the person having lawful control</strong>.</p>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 15</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q141–Q150) on Exfiltration, Navjot Sandhu, ISO 17025, and BSA Sec 63(2)(a).</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(14)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q141–Q150) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 15,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 31,
        "runningHead": "BSA Sec 63(2)(c), CFCFRMS & Section 43(e)-(j) Invasions",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 16 • Operating Integrity & Civil Contraventions</span>\n      <h2>BSA Sec 63(2)(c), CFCFRMS & Section 43(e)-(j) Invasions</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>BSA 2023 Section 63(2)(c) Operating Properly Condition (Q151)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 63(2)(c) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023</strong>, secondary electronic output is admissible only if: <strong>the computer was operating properly or, if out of operation, it did not affect the accuracy of the electronic record</strong>.</p>\n\n    <h3>CFCFRMS Real-Time Tracking Platform (Q152)</h3>\n    <p>The <strong>Citizen Financial Cyber Fraud Reporting and Management System (CFCFRMS)</strong> (Helpline 1930) operates as an integrated real-time platform linking police and financial intermediaries to track and freeze defrauded funds across banking switches before cash-out.</p>\n\n    <h3>Section 43 Sub-Clauses (e), (h), (i), (j)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 43(e) (Q153):</strong> Disrupting or causing disruption of any computer, computer system or computer network.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 43(h) (Q154):</strong> Charging services availed of by a person to the account of another person by tampering with computer resources.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 43(i) (Q155):</strong> Destroying, deleting or altering any information residing in a computer resource or diminishing its value or utility.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 43(j) (Q156):</strong> Stealing, concealing, destroying or altering or causing any person to steal, conceal, destroy or alter any computer source code with intent to cause damage.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 32,
        "runningHead": "Section 66 Options, Section 67 Obscenity, Sec 68 & Sec 69A(3)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Criminal Sanctions & Blocking Penalties</span>\n      <h2>Section 66 Options, Section 67 Obscenity, Sec 68 & Sec 69A(3)</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Criminal Penalties & Obscenity Threshold</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 66 Criminal Sentencing (Q157):</strong> If an act under Section 43 is committed dishonestly or fraudulently, Section 66 provides for <strong>imprisonment up to 3 years, OR with fine up to ₹5 Lakhs, OR with both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 67 Obscenity Threshold (Q158):</strong> Statutory test defines obscene material as <strong>material which is lascivious or appeals to the prurient interest or tends to deprave and corrupt</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 68 Directions Penalty (Q159):</strong> Failure to comply with directions of the Controller carries <strong>imprisonment up to 2 years, or fine up to ₹1 Lakh (or ₹2 Lakhs), or both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 69A(3) Takedown Failure (Q160):</strong> An intermediary who fails to comply with website blocking directions issued under Section 69A is punishable with <strong>imprisonment for a term which may extend to 7 years, and liability to fine</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 16</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q151–Q160) on BSA Sec 63(2)(c), CFCFRMS, Section 43(e)-(j), and Section 69A(3).</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(15)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q151–Q160) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 16,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 33,
        "runningHead": "Review Committee, 60-Day Interception & CERT-In Fines",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 17 • Interception Oversight & Bail Classification</span>\n      <h2>Review Committee, 60-Day Interception & CERT-In Fines</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Interception & Blocking Oversight (Q161-Q163)</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Review Committee (Q161):</strong> Under the Blocking Rules 2009, an independent Review Committee headed by the <strong>Cabinet Secretary of the Government of India</strong> meets at least once every 2 months to review blocking orders.</li>\n      <li><strong>Initial Interception Validity (Q162):</strong> Under the Interception Rules 2009, an interception order is valid for an initial period <strong>not exceeding 60 days</strong> (extendable up to an aggregate maximum of 180 days).</li>\n      <li><strong>Emergency Interception Authorization (Q163):</strong> In emergent situations where prior approval cannot be obtained, an interception order under Section 69 can be authorized by <strong>an officer not below Joint Secretary rank, subject to confirmation by the Home Secretary within 7 working days</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>CERT-In Criminal Penalty (Section 70B(7)) (Q164):</strong> Failure to comply with CERT-In directions is punishable with <strong>imprisonment up to 1 year, or fine up to ₹1 Crore (or ₹1 Lakh), or both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 76 Confiscation Separation (Q165):</strong> In network offenses, <strong>the court may separate and confiscate only the offending parts or data, protecting innocent network parts</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 34,
        "runningHead": "Section 77B Bail Matrix, Christian Louboutin & Firojuddin",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Bail Classification & Landmark Rulings</span>\n      <h2>Section 77B Bail Matrix, Christian Louboutin & Firojuddin</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Section 77B Bail Classification</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 66 (Hacking) (Q166):</strong> <strong>Cognizable and Bailable</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 67A (Sexually Explicit Acts) (Q167):</strong> <strong>Cognizable and Non-Bailable</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 66C (Identity Theft) (Q168):</strong> <strong>Cognizable and Bailable</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Landmark Judicial Precedents</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Christian Louboutin SAS v. Nakul Bajaj (2018) (Q169):</strong> The Delhi High Court held that <strong>an e-commerce platform that actively promotes, manages inventory, and profits from counterfeits loses Section 79 safe harbour protection</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Firojuddin v. State of Rajasthan (2023) (Q170):</strong> The High Court clarified that <strong>failure to produce the certificate along with the initial charge-sheet does NOT render it inadmissible; the prosecution can cure this by producing it at any time prior to evidence closing</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 17</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q161–Q170) on Review Committee, Section 77B bail classification, and Christian Louboutin.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(16)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q161–Q170) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 17,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 35,
        "runningHead": "Cryptographic Definitions (Section 2)",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 18 • Comprehensive Definitional Lexicon</span>\n      <h2>Cryptographic Definitions (Section 2)</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Cryptographic Lexicon Under Section 2</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Subscriber (Section 2(1)(zg)):</strong> A person in whose name the Electronic Signature Certificate is issued.</li>\n      <li><strong>Verify (Section 2(1)(zh)):</strong> In relation to a digital signature, means to determine whether the digital signature was created using the private key corresponding to the public key of the subscriber and whether the electronic record has been altered.</li>\n      <li><strong>Private Key (Section 2(1)(zc)):</strong> The key of a key pair used to create a digital signature.</li>\n      <li><strong>Public Key (Section 2(1)(zd)):</strong> The key of a key pair used to verify a digital signature and listed in the Digital Signature Certificate.</li>\n      <li><strong>Asymmetric Cryptosystem (Section 2(1)(c)):</strong> A system of a secure key pair consisting of a private key for creating a digital signature and a public key to verify the digital signature.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 36,
        "runningHead": "Section 2 Technical & Communication Definitions",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Device, Record & Cyber Cafe Definitions</span>\n      <h2>Section 2 Technical & Communication Definitions</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Statutory Definitions Under Section 2</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Communication Device (Section 2(1)(ha)):</strong> Cell phones, personal digital assistance (PDAs), or combination of both or any other device used to transmit any text, video, audio or image.</li>\n      <li><strong>Data (Section 2(1)(o)):</strong> A representation of information, knowledge, facts, concepts or instructions which are being prepared or have been prepared in a formalized manner.</li>\n      <li><strong>Electronic Record (Section 2(1)(t)):</strong> Data, record or data generated, image or sound stored, received or sent in an electronic form or micro film or computer generated micro fiche.</li>\n      <li><strong>Information (Section 2(1)(v)):</strong> Includes data, message, text, images, sound, voice, codes, computer programmes, software and databases or micro film or computer generated micro fiche.</li>\n      <li><strong>Cyber Cafe (Section 2(1)(m)):</strong> Any facility from where access to the internet is offered by any person in the ordinary course of business to the members of the public.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 18</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q171–Q180) on Complete Section 2 cryptographic and technical definitions.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(17)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q171–Q180) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 18,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 37,
        "runningHead": "Cyber Cafe Rules 2011 & Cyber Security Defined",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 19 • Cyber Cafe Operations & Cyber Security</span>\n      <h2>Cyber Cafe Rules 2011 & Cyber Security Defined</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Cyber Cafe Rules 2011 Compliance Obligations</h3>\n    <p>Under the <strong>Information Technology (Guidelines for Cyber Cafe) Rules, 2011</strong>, every cyber cafe owner must:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Identify users by inspecting <strong>government-issued photo identity documents</strong> and maintain an identity register before granting computer access;</li>\n      <li>Store physical user log registers and computer system/history logs for a <strong>minimum period of 1 year</strong>;</li>\n      <li>Ensure minors are not exposed to inappropriate adult content through commercial content filters.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Cyber Security Defined (Section 2(1)(na))</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 2(1)(na)</strong>, <strong>cyber security</strong> means <em>protecting information, equipment, devices, computer, computer resource, communication device and information stored therein from unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification or destruction</em>.</p>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 38,
        "runningHead": "Section 66E Penalty, CII Notifications & Section 75 Scope",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Statutory Authority & Extraterritoriality</span>\n      <h2>Section 66E Penalty, CII Notifications & Section 75 Scope</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Penalties & Statutory Appointments</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 66E (Voyeurism Penalty):</strong> On a first conviction, punishable with <strong>imprisonment up to 3 years, or fine up to ₹2 Lakhs, or both</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 66F(1)(B) (Essential Supplies Disruption):</strong> Acts causing disruption of supplies or services essential to the life of the community constitute <strong>Cyber Terrorism (punishable with imprisonment for life)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 70(1) (CII Authority):</strong> Declared by the <strong>appropriate Government</strong> (Central Government or State Government).</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 70B(1) (CERT-In Authority):</strong> Director-General and officers appointed by the <strong>Central Government</strong> by notification in the Official Gazette.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 72 (Breach Elements):</strong> Requires that the accused secured access to the electronic record <strong>pursuant to powers conferred under this Act or rules</strong> without consent.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 72A (Wrongful Loss/Gain):</strong> Requires that disclosure in breach of lawful contract was made with <strong>intent to cause wrongful loss or wrongful gain</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 75 (Extraterritoriality):</strong> Applies to Indian citizens or foreigners committing cyber offences outside India <strong>if the act involves a computer or network located in India</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 19</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q181–Q190) on Cyber Cafe Rules 2011, Section 66E penalty, and Section 75 scope.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(18)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q181–Q190) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    },
    {
      "spreadId": 19,
      "left": {
        "pageNumber": 39,
        "runningHead": "Confiscation, Safe Harbour Neutrality & Magistrate Production",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Chapter 20 • Evidence Admissibility & Forensic Proof</span>\n      <h2>Confiscation, Safe Harbour Neutrality & Magistrate Production</h2>\n    </div>\n    \n    <h3>Innocent Property Protection (Section 76) & Compounding Bar (Section 77A)</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 76</strong>, computer media of innocent third parties <strong>shall not be confiscated if the court is satisfied that the owner was innocent</strong> and had no knowledge. Under <strong>Section 77A</strong>, an offence punishable under Section 66F (Cyber Terrorism) <strong>CANNOT be compounded</strong> as it affects the socio-economic condition and sovereignty of the nation.</p>\n\n    <h3>Operational Neutrality of Intermediaries (Section 79(2))</h3>\n    <p>To claim safe harbour protection, an intermediary must meet two strict statutory conditions:</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 79(2)(a):</strong> Its function is <strong>limited strictly to providing access to a communication system</strong> over which information is transmitted or hosted; and</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 79(2)(b):</strong> The intermediary <strong>does not initiate the transmission, select the receiver, or select or modify the information contained in the transmission</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Post-Arrest Procedure (Section 80(2))</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 80(2)</strong>, any person arrested without a warrant by an authorized police officer shall be <strong>produced immediately before a Magistrate having jurisdiction or before the officer in charge of a police station</strong> without unnecessary delay.</p>\n\n    <h3>Corporate Defence Under Section 85</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Section 85(1):</strong> A director or manager avoids criminal liability if they prove that the contravention took place <strong>without their knowledge or that they exercised all due diligence to prevent it</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Section 85(2):</strong> Proof that the offense was committed with the <strong>consent, connivance of, or was attributable to any neglect on the part of</strong> any director or manager renders them directly liable to prosecution.</li>\n    </ul>"
      },
      "right": {
        "pageNumber": 40,
        "runningHead": "Chain of Custody, Hashing & Section 63(4) Certificates",
        "content": "<div class=\"legal-dispatch-header\">\n      <span class=\"badge-primary\">Forensic Integrity & Section 63 BSA</span>\n      <h2>Chain of Custody, Hashing & Section 63(4) Certificates</h2>\n    </div>\n\n    <h3>Chain of Custody & Cryptographic Baseline at Crime Scenes</h3>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Broken Chain of Custody (CoC) (Q198):</strong> In digital forensics, any broken chain of custody <strong>raises reasonable doubt as to whether the digital evidence was tampered with, replaced, or contaminated during transit or storage</strong>, rendering it legally vulnerable in court.</li>\n      <li><strong>Cryptographic Hashing (SHA-256) (Q199):</strong> Computing a cryptographic hash at the scene of electronic evidence collection <strong>establishes a mathematical baseline of evidence integrity to prove in court that the duplicate bit-stream image is mathematically identical to the seized source</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n    <h3>Section 63(4) Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam Dual Certificate</h3>\n    <p>Under <strong>Section 63(4) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023</strong>, admitting secondary electronic records requires two contemporaneous certificates:</p>\n    <ol>\n      <li><strong>Part A:</strong> Signed by the person in lawful charge of the computer device or management of the relevant activity certifying lawful custody and normal operational condition.</li>\n      <li><strong>Part B:</strong> Signed by an authorized forensic expert or Examiner of Electronic Evidence certifying the cryptographic extraction, hashing, and forensic tool parameters.</li>\n    </ol>\n\n    <div class=\"exam-trap-box\">\n      <strong>DSSSB Exam Trap:</strong> Note the twin pillars of forensic admissibility:\n      <ul>\n        <li>Mathematical baseline: <strong>SHA-256 hash computed at crime scene</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Evidentiary certificate: <strong>Section 63(4) BSA (Part A & Part B)</strong>.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    \n        <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-banner\">\n          <div class=\"spread-quiz-cta-info\">\n            <span style=\"font-size: 1.1rem; margin-right: 8px;\">🎯</span>\n            <div>\n              <strong style=\"font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--text-heading);\">Test Your Mastery: Spread 20</strong>\n              <div style=\"font-size: 0.74rem; color: var(--text-muted);\">Practice all 10 targeted exam questions (Q191–Q200) on Chain of custody, SHA-256 integrity, and Section 63 BSA.</div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <button class=\"btn-tool btn-mcq\" onclick=\"window.openMCQForSpread(19)\" style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; padding: 6px 14px;\">Start Quiz (Q191–Q200) →</button>\n        </div>"
      }
    }
